{"id":"10.1016/j.snb.2024.135645","venue":"j-fe57259a51","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"A bacterial biosensor based on gold nanoparticles functionalized by a hydrophobin-chimera and combined with machine learning for user-friendly detection","authors":["Anna Pennacchio","Fabio Giampaolo","Valeria Cafaro","Paola Cicatiello","Bartolomeo Della Ventura","Paola Giardina","Rosario Rosanova","Martina Savoia","Raffaele Velotta","Francesco Piccialli","Alessandra Piscitelli"],"author_ids":[],"abstract":"A chimeric protein, in which a cationic antimicrobial peptide was fused to the adhesive self-assembling moiety of a fungal class I hydrophobin, was produced and used to easily functionalize gold nanoparticles (f-AuNPs). f-AuNPs are able to bind Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus epidermidis bacteria, tested as model organisms. A reverse colorimetric assay was then set up based on the induced aggregation of the free f-AuNPs. This leads to changes in optical properties of the solution, visible even by naked eyes. This approach allows detection of 10 CFU mL-1 of E. coli within 15 min and can be applicable to detect bacterial contamination in real matrices. Notably, the colorimetric variation was combined to machine-learning methodologies inferring data from photos of the colored solutions, acquired by smartphones. This fast, highly practical and flexible system, would enable on-site measurements and, therefore, help to prevent significant contaminations.","keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.snb.2024.135645","pages":"135645","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.snb.2024.135645","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-07","source":"crossref.org+openalex.org","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Sensors and Actuators B: Chemical","issn":"0925-4005","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_article_type","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":nu